<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juliana of Stolberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1506">1506</a>–<a href="/years/1580">1580</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juliana of Stolberg was a German countess, daughter of Count Botho III of Stolberg, who married William, Count of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a>, in 1531. Of her many children, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-silent">William the Silent</a> led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-revolt">Dutch Revolt</a> and his brothers Louis, Adolf and Henry died in its cause, while John VI of Nassau-Dillenburg became a Calvinist leader. She is remembered as the mother of the house of Orange-Nassau.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juliana-of-stolberg.jpg" alt="Juliana of Stolberg" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portret_van_Juliana_van_Stolberg_(1506-1580).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Juliana was born on 15 February 1506 in Stolberg into the House of Stolberg; her mother was Countess Anna of Eppstein-Königstein. She was raised a Roman Catholic. In 1523 she married Philip II of Hanau-Münzenberg, who died in 1529, and in just under six years she bore him five children. The first, Reinhard, lived two days. Philip III became Count of Hanau-Münzenberg, a second Reinhard died in battle on 11 October 1554 serving in the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the daughters Catherine and Juliana married into the houses of Wied-Runkel and Salm-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyrburg">Kyrburg</a>.</p><p>Her second marriage, on 20 September 1531, brought twelve children in eighteen years: William I of Orange, John VI the Elder, Louis, Adolf and Henry, a daughter Hermanna who died young, and six daughters who married into the comital houses of Berg, Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weilburg">Weilburg</a>, Solms-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braunfels">Braunfels</a>, Schwarzburg and Hohenlohe. Juliana changed her faith twice, first to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>, and with her husband, also a convinced Protestant, she raised the children in the Protestant faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After her husband died in 1559 she went on living at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> castle, which then belonged to her son John. She stayed close to her children throughout her life, above all to William. When he rose against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-spain">Philip II of Spain</a> she supported him both morally and with money, and that support allowed him to campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>.</p><p>Juliana died on 18 June 1580. She had borne 17 children in all and left 123 grandchildren. Her life was retold as fiction by Ethel Herr in Dr. Oma: The Healing Wisdom of Countess Juliana Von Stolberg, published in 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juliana of Stolberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Count Botho III's daughter <b>Juliana of Stolberg</b> was mother of William the Silent, and his sons introduced the Reformation after his death in 1538.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-stolberg">County of Stolberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Stolberg (Harz), in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.57,10.95&amp;year=1554" aria-label="Stolberg (Harz) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Juliana of Stolberg"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5579 5671h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5579 5671h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.57,10.95&amp;year=1554">What was here in 1554? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
